THE PORTAL BASE
- A portal base is a ring-shaped station that hangs fixed over the star of its system, on the star's z axis, about 10 heat radii from it (the distance at which a ship starts to heat up is the heat radius). It does not orbit. It keeps itself in place, against the pull of the star. It is common knowledge that the bases sit "over the stars".
- It has usually a hangar on the axis (which does not spin) joined to the ring by four spokes with lifts. The mouth of the hangar faces the star. The inside of the hangar is where the ships dock: arms move them once inside and park them in the rear part. It is heavily defended with missiles and lasers, retracted unless a threat comes.

- They come in different sizes. for the smallest ones, its  ring spins to simulate a tenth of the gravity of the Earth on its floor. Central systems have larger structures. Those important central systems may have a second portal base on the other side of the star, under it (down from the sun), for redundancy. Not every system has a base, but all the inhabited ones do.

- Different political entities use different configurations and names for each size.
    - In the case of Those-Who-Share, for small stations, it is 100 m to 200 m of ring radius in steps of 5 m. Its names start with "Protecting-" followed by a emotion, behaviour or attitude defended by the ideology, like "Solidarity". In Those-Who-Share, a small portal base will have: communication officers, dockmasters, stevedores (two teams working at the same time), gunners (without substitutes), janitors and security, in teams that rotate in three shifts of 8 hours, plus two people always on duty: a Komisar and a Ringmaster. The Ringmaster is the chief of the base and thus the leader of the whole Community in a freshly developing system, with responsibility over the Coordinators of any outpost there. The Komisar analyses, encourages and eventually ensures ideological alignment in that stellar system.

- Portal bases offer largely the same services than outposts, and more.